From 75e643a012f83f19e835b9adcfbff05fae86cdc8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeffrey Frederick <5095066+jeffreyfrederick@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 20:51:41 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] winit: lock the frame-rate limit to the display's real
 refresh rate

Problem
-------
The frame-rate limit (set_max_frame_rate) schedules frames on fixed slots
at exactly the limit, e.g. 60.000 Hz. A "60 Hz" display really refreshes
at 59.94-59.96 Hz, and winit reports the mode's rounded 60.0. The two
clocks beat against each other: every ~20 s the slots slide across a
vblank boundary and the display shows a 5-10 s stretch of alternating
1- and 2-vblank frames (visible stutter). With a 120 limit on that display
every other frame is wasted.

Fix
---
1. Precise refresh rate. display_refresh_rate() now refines winit's
   rounded rate with the platform's precise one when they agree within
   1 Hz: CVDisplayLinkGetNominalOutputVideoRefreshPeriod for the window's
   CGDirectDisplayID on macOS, DwmGetCompositionTimingInfo on Windows. It
   is re-read when the window moves or changes scale, and once a second.

2. Slot rate (frame_slot_rate, public, documented). If refresh/limit is
   within 2% (FRAME_SLOT_RATE_TOLERANCE) of a whole number n >= 1, the
   slots run at exactly refresh/n (one frame every n vblanks); a refresh
   rate at or below the limit gives refresh. Otherwise the slots stay at
   the limit as before, so e.g. a 60 limit on a 320 Hz display keeps its
   5/5/6-vblank cadence. Locking may exceed the limit by up to 2%.

3. Phase (macOS). With the rates matched, the slot phase no longer drifts
   quickly, but it can sit on a vblank boundary, and the display's real
   rate still differs from its nominal one by tens of ppm. While the slots
   are locked, a CVDisplayLink is started every 10 s for ~6 vblanks, only
   to read their host timestamps, then stopped (it never drives
   rendering). Slots are aligned to 0.85 of a vblank after the latest
   lattice point; this puts the GL command-buffer commit mid-interval.
   A freshly started link is anchored to the hardware; a link left
   running follows the nominal rate and drifts by up to 3-4 ms before
   correcting itself, which is why it is restarted instead of kept on.
   On a move or scale change the phase is re-read at once.

Default: no limit, so nothing changes unless an app opts in.

Tested
------
macOS 26.6, Apple M1, 60 Hz display (nominal 59.9505 Hz, measured
59.953 Hz) and a 320 Hz display, Metal System Trace displayed surfaces,
120 s runs:
  60 Hz, limit 60:  frames > 20 ms 230 -> 6, steady commit phase
                    (0.52-0.54 of a vblank, sd 0.03)
  60 Hz, limit 120: 120 fps -> 59.95 fps (one per vblank), 753 -> 412 mW
  320 Hz, limit 60 / 120: cadence unchanged (5/5/6, 2/3), instructions
                    per frame within 0.5%
  window moved between the displays: the rate follows each move
The remaining slow frames are isolated 3-vblank holds that occur at the
same rate without this change.

Not tested: the Windows path (DwmGetCompositionTimingInfo) has been
neither compiled nor run; it needs a Windows build before merging.

